rtc: Retu RTC as a retu-mfd cell

The N8x0 had no working RTC: Menelaus's finds no 32 kHz oscillator on
the board, and mainline never had a driver for Retu's (Nokia's lived in
drivers/cbus in the linux-omap and Maemo trees), so the clock came from
a saved file and NTP.

Ported from Nokia's 2.6.21 retu-rtc.c (GPL, Copyright (C) 2004, 2005
Nokia Corporation, Paul Mundt, Igor Stoppa) to an rtc class driver
(CONFIG_RTC_DRV_RETU, "retu-rtc", a retu-mfd cell without a DT node, so
both DTBs are unchanged; the N800's Retu gets it too). relghuar's
rtc-retu.c (linux-stable-nokia-tablets) was not a usable base: it takes
the date from the system clock, puts the day counter in tm_mday and
writes RTCHMR, which Nokia never wrote.

The hardware, as Nokia's driver used it: seconds (RTCDSR[5:0]), minutes
(RTCHMR[5:0]) and hours (RTCHMR[12:8]) run free and wrap at 24 h into an
8-bit day counter (RTCDSR[15:8]) that sticks at 255. Any write to RTCDSR
clears the day counter (bit 6, temperature shutdown, written back);
hours, minutes and seconds can't be set. The counters are read as Nokia
did (each register twice, DSR again after HMR until it agrees).

The date base: time = base + counter, counter = days * 86400 +
h * 3600 + m * 60 + s. set_time() clears the day counter and keeps
base = time - counter (s since 1970, UTC, 64 bits) in memory;
read_time() returns base + counter. The range is 254 days plus up to
24 h after the last set_time(); after that the day counter is stuck at
255 and read_time() fails (-EINVAL), as it does while no base is known.
Resolution is 1 s (the seconds phase is not settable). The rtc's range
is 2000..9999.

The base doesn't survive a reboot, and Retu has nowhere to keep it: as
with Nokia's driver, whose sysfs "time" gave userspace the raw counters,
userspace keeps the date. The rtc device gets two attributes:
- base (rw): the base; read -ENODATA while unknown. Saved by userspace
  after setting the RTC, written back at boot before hwclock -s.
- counter (ro): the counter in seconds.
A base write is accepted only while RTCHMAR's alarm field holds 24:60
(alarm off, as Nokia's driver wrote it on disabling an alarm), which
set_time() writes (RTC watchdog bits kept). Nokia's reset after a power
loss, done at probe when RTCCALR reads 0 (CC1 bit 0 pulsed, RTCHMAR
0x7f3f, RTCCALR 0x00c0), leaves 31:63 there, so a base saved before the
reset is refused (-ESTALE). RTCHMAR and RTCCALR writes wait for a
seconds tick (Nokia: they must come within 0.9 s of one); the seconds
counter is polled for it instead of taking Retu's RTCS interrupt.

So HCTOSYS finds no base at boot ("unable to read the hardware clock");
S02clock restores it and runs hwclock -s. SYSTOHC must stay off: its
writes clear the day counter behind userspace's saved base.

Not ported: alarms (RTCA; Nokia's alarm, alarm_expired), the calibration
and reset sysfs files, the RTC watchdog.

QEMU's Retu model returns 0 for RTCDSR/RTCHMR/RTCHMAR and ignores
writes to them (cbus.c: "TODO"), so QEMU tests only the plumbing: on
n810 and n800 the driver probes as rtc0 (hctosys: no base); hwclock -r
fails until hwclock -w, then reads back what was written (the counter
stays 0, "seconds counter not running" once); a base write is refused
(RTCHMAR stays 0). Not on hardware.

